3:1-18 (* Lex is the M9 lexer, in M9: the first thousandth of the\n   self-hosted compiler (P5 stage 1).  It is restated from\n   host/fpc/M9Lex.pas, which now serves as its differential oracle:\n   both must emit identical token streams over every corpus and\n   museum file.\n   Kinds are I64 codes, stable and documented: 0 EOF, 1 Error,\n   2 Ident, 3 IntLit, 4 RealLit, 5 CharLit, 6 StrLit, then keywords\n   from 7 and operators from 200.  Codes are identifiers, not\n   positions in a sorted list: a new keyword APPENDS at the next\n   free code rather than renumbering its alphabetical neighbours.\n   Errors are tokens carrying their message; lexing continues.\n   Lex is STATEFUL for the comment side channel's list (Collect,\n   below).  The unexpected-character message was the other reason, a\n   module buffer until 2026-10-23.  The source is UTF-8 (step 4 of\n   docs/utf8-stage3-plan.md): Init takes a STR and encodes it, InitUtf\n   takes the octets as they are.                                     *)
23:23-23 (* verbatim source; for Error, message *)
26:3-29 (* the source is UTF-8 and pos a BYTE offset into it (since\n     2026-10-23, docs/utf8-stage3-plan.md step 4); line and col count\n     characters, as they always did, so a diagnostic's position is the\n     same on both sides of the switch *)
35:3-39 (* A Comment holds a comment, for the callers that want the\n     documentation rather than the program.  text is VERBATIM and\n     includes the opening `(*` and closing `*)`, because a reader that\n     wants them stripped can strip them and one that wants the nesting\n     cannot put them back. *)
46:3-48 (* Init encodes src into the lexer's own pool: the STR form, for a\n     caller that has not moved to UTF.  Lex.Init (lx, u) with a UTF is\n     InitUtf. *)
51:3-52 (* The lexer keeps the source; keep it alive while lexing.  That is\n     the Json.Parse contract again.  Nothing is copied. *)
55:3-68 (* Next answers the next token, skipping blanks and nested comments\n     on the way.\n\n     ERRORS ARE TOKENS: a bad character or an unterminated comment\n     comes back as an Error token carrying the message and its\n     line:col, and lexing CONTINUES.  There is no error state to\n     check and no exception to handle, so a caller can report every\n     mistake in a file in one pass, which is what lextest does.\n\n       t -- VAR rather than a result because a Token is a record and\n            this is called once per token; the caller reuses one.\n\n     Comments leave no trace here at all: they cost zero tokens, so\n     a file's token count is a count of code.                      *)
71:3-72 (* KindName answers the FPC oracle's names, for dumps and\n     diagnostics. *)
75:3-77 (* IsKeyword answers whether w spells a keyword, in capitals.  M9f\n     asks it to reserve each keyword's lower-case .m9f form, so a\n     keyword appended to the table is reserved there too. *)
79:1-101 (* ---- comments, BESIDE the token stream ----\n\n   Comments are not tokens and never will be: making one a token\n   would change every file's token stream and put a code in the\n   kind table that the parser must then be taught to ignore.  So\n   `Skip` throws them away, which is right for a compiler and leaves\n   nothing for a documentation tool to read.\n\n   These three procedures are the side channel.  Armed, the lexer\n   ALSO appends each comment it skips to a module-level list; the\n   token stream, the counts and the printer are untouched, and a\n   caller that never arms it pays one BOOL test per comment.\n\n   Storage is HEAP, the predeclared pool: the list outlives the\n   Lexer that filled it (a caller reads it after parsing, not\n   during), and Init takes no pool to borrow.  It is therefore\n   never freed, which is what HEAP means, and is the same bargain\n   every other one-module-per-process registry here makes.\n\n   THE TEXT IS A VIEW, not a copy: a Comment's text is a slice of\n   the source Init was given, so the source must stay alive for as\n   long as the comments are read.  That is the contract Init already\n   states, extended past the last token. *)
104:3-108 (* Collect arms or disarms.  Arming also CLEARS what was collected, so\n     one process can lex several files and keep them apart; disarming\n     keeps it, so a caller can arm, parse one file, disarm, and then\n     read the comments of that file only while its dependencies go past\n     uncollected.  That is exactly what m9c --doc needs.              *)
112:3-118 (* ComCount and ComAt answer what was collected, in source\n     order.\n\n       i -- 0 .. ComCount - 1.  An UNTERMINATED comment is not in\n            here at all: it never closed, so it has no end line and\n            no text, and the Error token Next answers is the whole\n            of what is known about it.                              *)
125:1-126 (* UNSAFE is inherited style only: nothing foreign here yet; ADR is\n   unused.  Drop UNSAFE the day the checker minds.                   *)
139:3-148 (* Kind codes are stable IDENTIFIERS, not positions in a sorted\n     list.  The first 58 keywords happen to run alphabetically\n     because they arrived together; every later one APPENDS at the\n     next free code and the table stops being alphabetical.  That is\n     deliberate: deriving a code from spelling means an unrelated\n     keyword renumbers its neighbours, which churns both lexers, the\n     parser's token table and every recorded stream.  A code that\n     moves is a code no one can rely on.  Lookup is a linear scan,\n     so alphabetical order buys nothing but the reading.\n     Keywords own 7..199 and grow upward; operators start at 200.   *)
150:27-150 (* grows as keywords are appended *)
162:3-164 (* Ascii holds the ASCII characters by code: a byte below 128 is\n     read as its CHAR with no conversion to check.  CHR would put a\n     RAISES ValueRange on every Peek. *)
180:33-180 (* Collect armed? *)
181:33-181 (* grown in HEAP, doubling *)
201:1-207 (* Note puts one comment onto the list, doubling the vector when it\n   is full.\n   The abandoned vector stays in HEAP, which is the same trade\n   DynStr.Grow makes and for the same reason.  The text is COPIED\n   into HEAP too: a view of the source would be module state keeping\n   the lexer's input, and every lexer up to the parser's callers\n   would have to say KEPT for a comment list (par 4.1, 2026-10-18). *)
290:3-291 (* These are appended after the alphabetical 58: codes are\n     identifiers. *)
352:1-354 (* At answers the character the byte at i of u is, for an ASCII run\n   (digits, names): 0C for a byte past ASCII, which no such run\n   holds. *)
363:1-364 (* SpellsKw answers whether u, an identifier's octets, spells the\n   keyword s. *)
396:3-400 (* a first line beginning #! is the operating system's, naming the\n     program that runs this file as a script (`#!/usr/bin/m9c --run`),\n     and is not M9 (report par 2).  It is skipped to its line end, not\n     past it, so the newline is counted and every line keeps its\n     number. *)
401:3-404 (* a byte order mark (U+FEFF), which some editors write first, is\n     not M9 either: it is skipped, and the column stays 1.  It was\n     refused as `unexpected character 0xff', its low byte (the\n     review's F6). *)
418:1-420 (* Peek answers the character ahead characters on from pos (0 the one\n   at pos); 0C past the end.  An ASCII byte is its character; past\n   ASCII the character is decoded. *)
445:3-446 (* a line ends at LF, and at a CR no LF follows: a file with CR-only\n     line ends was one line (the review's F6) *)
453:3-454 (* Advance moves past the character: one byte, or the octets of one\n     past ASCII. *)
483:1-484 (* Skip skips blanks and nested comments; bad=TRUE means an\n   unterminated comment opened at eline:ecol. *)
512:13-518 (* lx.line is still the line the closing delimiter\n               sits on: advancing past it cannot have crossed a\n               newline.  And note that this sentence may not name\n               that delimiter literally -- comments NEST, so the two\n               characters would close this one early.  That is a\n               real constraint on writing M9 prose, and it cost 1900\n               parse errors to rediscover. *)
550:3-550 (* Two-char lookahead forms go first: longest match. *)
601:1-612 (* ---- literals beyond 64 bits (2026-10-21, the compiler review's F1).\n   A literal the lexer accepted was any length.\n   `18446744073709551617' reached C as an integer constant too large\n   for its type, which gcc truncates with a warning (the program\n   printed 1), and a real literal past F64's range became an\n   infinity.  Both lexers refuse them here, where the digits are.  An\n   integer literal is at most 64 bits, decimal or hex (one past\n   MAX (I64) is a U64, which the checker types it as).  A real must\n   not round to infinity as an F64, held EXACTLY: it does when its\n   value is at least 2^1024 - 2^970, whose 309 digits are F64Top\n   (written by Python's int, round to nearest even sends that value\n   itself to infinity). ---- *)
617:1-618 (* Significant answers the digits of a run with its leading zeros\n   dropped. *)
627:1-628 (* IntPast64 asks whether a decimal integer literal is past 64\n   bits. *)
641:1-644 (* RealTooBig asks whether a real literal `ddd.ddd' with an optional\n   exponent rounds to infinity as an F64.  Its value is 0.D * 10^n\n   with D its digits, the leading zeros moved into n, compared with\n   F64Top digit by digit. *)
665:7-666 (* The exponent is capped: E999999999999999999 is past every\n         bound, and its value must not overflow the counting. *)
678:48-678 (* zero *)
690:3-691 (* the literal's digits ran out first: it is below the threshold\n     unless every digit of the threshold left is a zero *)
728:3-728 (* digits and uppercase hex letters; classify by shape *)
775:3-775 (* the 1..5 case: dot followed by dot is DOTDOT, not a real *)
835:5-836 (* Peek answers 0C past the end; a NUL before it is a character,\n       and ended the source without a word (the review's F6) *)
883:3-885 (* The message 'unexpected character 0xNN' is composed as a UTF in\n     the frame and adopted with the token.  It was a module buffer\n     until 2026-10-23, the reason Lex was STATEFUL then. *)
890:5-892 (* This takes the digit as the octet it is (ASCII): the same both\n       sides of the UTF switch, where a subscript of a string CONST is\n       refused. *)
895:5-897 (* Past ASCII the message names the scalar, U+XXXX with at least\n       four digits: its low byte named another character (U+FEFF read\n       0xff). *)
comments=43
